            <ArticleTitle>Symptoms before, during, and 14 months after the beginning of treatment as perceived by patients with lymphoma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>E105-13</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1188/10.ONF.E105-E113</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE/OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To explore occurrence of symptoms and relationships between them as perceived by patients with lymphoma before, during, and 14 months after the beginning of treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESEARCH APPROACH" NlmCategory="METHODS">Qualitative and longitudinal.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">A major oncology center in the United Kingdom.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">10 adult patients with lymphoma (3 women and 7 men) were recruited at treatment initiation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODOLOGIC APPROACH" NlmCategory="METHODS">Semistructured audiotaped interviews were conducted with participants in median 15 days, 4 months, and 14 months after diagnosis. Analysis of the verbatim transcripts was inspired by interpretive description, which is a grounded approach articulating patterns emerging in relation to clinical phenomena.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN RESEARCH VARIABLES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Symptoms.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Symptoms commonly reported by patients in this sample were lack of energy, lymphadenopathy, weight loss, itching, pain, sadness, night sweats, sleeping difficulties, and hair loss. Co-occurring prediagnosis symptoms seem to have led patients to seek medical attention; co-occurring symptoms during treatment seem to have a cumulatively distressing effect. Several of the symptoms were described as interrelated, with one symptom leading to one or more other symptoms.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The data confirm a complex symptomatology in patients with lymphoma. In addition, the findings support that co-occurring symptoms may have a synergistic effect on patients' health outcomes and add new knowledge about relationships between symptoms from patients' perspectives.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERPRETATION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Illustrating symptoms and interrelationships between symptoms using diagrams may be useful to support communication as well as in identifying targets for symptom management.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
